  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1135 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough across the Western US and associated upper-\nlevel low over Southern CA will begin to traverse eastward through\nthe weekend. A series of weak upper-level disturbances will eject\nahead of the trough and pass through West Central Texas Saturday\nand Sunday, mainly bringing some cloudier skies and very low\nchances of a isolated shower or two to our western most counties.\nAlso, a tightening surface pressure gradient and nocturnal low- \nlevel jet development will support somewhat gusty south to \nsoutheast winds over the weekend. \n\nA slightly more potent upper-level disturbances is expected to \ntrack through the area late Monday morning, and will bring \nincreased shower and thunderstorm chances to West Central Texas \nthrough Tuesday morning. Shower and thunderstorms chances at this \ntime remain in the low to medium range (20-40 percent), with the \nhighest chances across the Big Country Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning. Models continue to show a cold front tracking through the\narea on Tuesday, keeping some lower (20-30 percent) showers and \nthunderstorm chances in the forecast through Tuesday. By Thursday \nmorning, models have started to hint at another round of \nprecipitation across the area persisting into the start of next \nweekend. However, confidence remains lower at this time due to \nongoing model uncertainty. \n\nTemperatures will remain overall pleasant and slightly above\nnormal, with morning lows in the mid 50s to mid 60s and afternoon\nhighs in the mid 70s to lower 80s through Sunday. Then, with \nincreasing cloud cover and precipitation chances limiting daytime \nhigh temperatures, temperatures will mostly remain in the 70s \nthrough next Friday.",
